Drive Hive Desktop directly over its local HTTP API, instead of — or alongside —
editing its config files.
{{template "app" .}}
This install also runs a loopback HTTP API you can call to observe and operate
the app: list and create profiles, read the inbox and feeds, set a profile
avatar, force a source refresh, and dry-run a flow against a payload you supply
— without editing the files above.
{{if not .Env.APIEnabled}}
NOTE: the server is currently **disabled** in this install's settings. Set
`http.enabled: true` in {{.Env.SettingsPath}} to turn it on; until then nothing
below will respond.
{{end}}
The API is self-describing, so you do not work from this prompt — you work from
the server. Two endpoints tell you everything it serves:

    {{if .Env.APIBaseURL}}{{.Env.APIBaseURL}}{{else}}<base-url>{{end}}/                # GET — a compact index of every route, one line each
    {{if .Env.APIBaseURL}}{{.Env.APIBaseURL}}{{else}}<base-url>{{end}}/openapi.json   # GET — the full OpenAPI 3.2 document (paths, schemas)

Fetch the OpenAPI document first and work from it. It is generated from the
server's own types, so it never drifts from what the server actually accepts,
and its `servers` entry is set to the address you fetched it from — so the base
URL is ready to use as-is.

Conventions the document assumes:

- Every response is JSON. Every error shares the shape `{"kind": "...", "message": "...", "fields"?: {...}}` — `kind` is a stable machine string (`invalid`, `not_found`, `conflict`, `unavailable`, ...) and `fields` carries per-field validation detail.
- Request bodies are JSON except where a `requestBody` says otherwise: the avatar upload (`PUT /api/profiles/{id}/image`) takes the raw image bytes as the body — PNG, JPEG, GIF, or WebP — not `multipart/form-data`.
- Reads and dry runs are safe to repeat; `POST /api/flows/execute` in particular commits nothing, so use it to test a flow edit — including one you have not saved — instead of deploying and waiting for a poll.
